# Limiting Nutrients
#
# The law of the minimum and the Redfield ratio in ecosystems, then four public-health histories told as cases, never as advice.

#>development_ecology_limiting_nutrients
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!development_ecology
#:form #!dossier
#:level #!intro
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bane

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Limiting Nutrients"
 ~#claim: "Growth stops at the scarcest element needed, which explains algal blooms in lakes and, as history, the deficiency diseases that whole populations once suffered."
 ~#proof: "The law of the minimum after Sprengel and Liebig; whole-lake phosphorus experiments from Schindler 1974; histories of scurvy, pellagra, goiter, and neural tube defects from Lind, Goldberger, Zimmermann, Wills, and the MRC trial."
 ~#door: "Read the lake first, then the four cases in date order, then try the jar experiment."
 ~#audience: "curious readers and students of ecology and public-health history"
 continuity: "Limiting Nutrients | the scarcest element"
 cold_open: "A lake grows only as far as the scarcest element allows, and whole populations once lived by the same rule."
}

